From 38350ed5b36263d9e77473f98fcb9e741ac951d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:17:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] serve: change-gate producer_admission sweep +
 memoize get_running_pid for profile lists (card J, part 1)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Design t_01a1d563324d7e8e §3, card J rows 1-2.

producer_admission.sweep_once opened every profile's SessionDB on every
15 s pass (13 of 17 profile samples; P1 on the hub serve: 1.9% of a core).
Each store now keeps one read-only autocommit observer connection; a pass
reads its (dev, ino, PRAGMA data_version) and skips the store only when
that key is unchanged since a pass that found nothing pending. A pending
row is due again on every pass (a refused resume or busy owner retries
next tick), so pending stores are never skipped; an unchanged-key store
with pending rows still takes today's full path. When the observer can
not answer (unreadable, replaced mid-read) the pass falls back to the
full path. Observers of profiles no longer listed are closed.

hermes_cli.profiles._check_gateway_running ran get_running_pid's flock
probe and command-line identity scan per profile on every polled profile
list. gateway/status_pid_memo.running_pid reuses the answer while the PID
and lock files keep their (dev, ino, mtime_ns, size), a cached live PID is
still the same live process (Linux: one /proc/<pid>/stat read, not a
zombie, same start ticks; elsewhere kill(pid,0) + start time + runtime
lock held), and at most RECHECK_S=30 s has passed. A racy read (files
moved during the probe) is answered but never memoized; answers are keyed
by the probe in effect.

Tests: RED on base 0ef3eae4ad7 (7 of 10 gate tests fail: reopen every
pass; replay bench 148-165 ms per pass = ~1% of a core vs budget 0.2%;
profiles memo test probes 5x). GREEN: change picked up next pass, pending
row retried each pass with no other change, RECHECK_S deadline reprobes
with no other change, dead/reused/zombie PID noticed with no file change;
replay benches assert 0.2% and 0.05% of a core.
